<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tetrarchy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">293 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tetrarchy was the system of imperial government established by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> in 293 CE, under which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> was ruled by four emperors: two senior Augusti and two junior Caesars. Each was responsible for a different region, allowing closer defence of the frontiers. The Tetrarchy and Constantine's reforms temporarily stabilised the empire after the crisis of the third century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tetrarchy.jpg" alt="Tetrarchy" width="280" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nino Barbieri · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Venice_%E2%80%93_The_Tetrarchs_03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The first phase, sometimes called the diarchy, began when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> made the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximian">Maximian</a> caesar in 285 and promoted him to augustus in 286. Diocletian took care of the east and Maximian of the west. In 293, thinking that civic and military problems needed more attention, Diocletian expanded the imperial college with Maximian's consent by appointing two caesares, one responsible to each augustus: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galerius">Galerius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantius-i">Constantius I</a>.</p><p>The word tetrarchy, from the Greek for leadership of four people, was never applied in antiquity to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a>'s college. It described independent portions of a kingdom, such as the tetrarchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judaea</a> after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-the-great">Herod the Great</a>. Diocletian's arrangement was a college led by one supreme leader. The term entered scholarship in 1887 and became current only after Otto Seeck used it in 1897.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximian">Maximian</a> abdicated on 1 May 305, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galerius">Galerius</a> and Constantius became augusti, with Valerius Severus and Maximinus Daza as new caesares. The system broke down quickly. When Constantius died in 306, his army proclaimed his son Constantine augustus. Maxentius, the son of Maximian, rebelled and defeated Severus, who was murdered in 307, and Maxentius and Maximian both declared themselves augusti. A conference at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carnuntum">Carnuntum</a> in 308 made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/licinius">Licinius</a> augustus in the west, but Constantine and Maximinus refused to accept a superior, and both were recognised as augusti in 309.</p><p>Constantine forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximian">Maximian</a>'s suicide in 310. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galerius">Galerius</a> died naturally in 311, Maxentius was defeated by Constantine at the Battle of the Milvian Bridge in 312 and killed, and Maximinus killed himself at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarsus">Tarsus</a> in 313. Two rulers remained until Constantine defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/licinius">Licinius</a> in 324 and became sole augustus. The fourfold division survived in the praetorian prefectures, and the idea of sharing imperial power recurred repeatedly.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The four tetrarchs based themselves in cities closer to the frontiers rather than in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, which stayed the nominal capital. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicomedia">Nicomedia</a> was the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirmium">Sirmium</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galerius">Galerius</a>, Mediolanum (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>) of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximian">Maximian</a> and Augusta Treverorum (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a>) of Constantius. There was no precise regional division, and the Roman state was never split into four sub-empires. Each emperor was often in the field, delegating most administration to a praetorian prefect.</p><p>The public image of the four was carefully managed to suggest a united empire, and official portraits and coins show them with identical features. The system was meant to solve the problem of the third century, when an emperor could command on only one front. Under it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galerius">Galerius</a> crushed the Persian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narseh">Narseh</a> in 298 and gained a treaty that kept peace for a generation, and Constantius defeated the British usurper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allectus">Allectus</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tetrarchy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Diocletian's <b>Tetrarchy</b> (293 CE) and Constantine's reforms temporarily stabilised the system, but the Battle of Adrianople in 378 CE, in which Emperor Valens was killed together with perhaps two-thirds of the Eastern field army by Gothic forces, was a turning point.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-empire">Roman Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The arrangement, called the <b>Tetrarchy</b>, &quot;rulership by four&quot;, divided the empire's lands and implied a line of succession.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-1">1 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The meeting aimed to end the civil wars of the <b>Tetrarchy</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-11">11 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>He defeated Carus's surviving son Carinus and later created the <b>Tetrarchy</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-20">20 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
